WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:04.000
In June nineteen ninety-one, Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines

00:04.000 --> 00:07.200
produced one of the largest eruptions of the twentieth century.

00:07.200 --> 00:10.297
Its ash cloud rose thirty-five kilometers into the sky.

00:10.297 --> 00:13.469
In Hawaii, people walk up to the edge of a lava flow to watch

00:13.469 --> 00:14.858
it. Both are volcanoes.

00:14.858 --> 00:18.379
The difference comes from where the magma forms, and what it is

00:18.379 --> 00:20.177
made of. The mantle is solid.

00:20.177 --> 00:22.574
To make magma, something has to change.

00:22.574 --> 00:25.211
Surprisingly, it is usually not extra heat.

00:25.211 --> 00:26.769
There are two other ways.

00:26.769 --> 00:28.567
One is to lower the pressure.

00:28.567 --> 00:32.342
When hot mantle rises, the pressure on it drops, and it partly

00:32.342 --> 00:36.477
melts. That is what happens at divergent boundaries and at hotspots.

00:36.477 --> 00:38.334
The other way is to add water.

00:38.334 --> 00:41.331
Water lowers the temperature at which rock melts.

00:41.331 --> 00:45.106
That is what happens at subduction zones, and it is the one to

00:45.106 --> 00:49.121
understand best, because it produces the most dangerous volcanoes.

00:49.121 --> 00:50.547
Follow the three steps.

00:50.547 --> 00:53.869
An ocean plate has spent millions of years under the sea, so its

00:53.869 --> 00:57.456
rocks hold water. As the plate sinks, it heats up and is squeezed,

00:57.456 --> 00:59.128
and the water is driven out.

00:59.128 --> 01:01.842
That water rises into the hot mantle above.

01:01.842 --> 01:05.023
It lowers the melting point, and part of the mantle melts.

01:05.023 --> 01:08.328
The magma is less dense than the rock around it, so it rises,

01:08.328 --> 01:11.062
and where it reaches the surface it builds a volcano.

01:11.062 --> 01:14.554
Because this happens all along the sinking plate, the volcanoes

01:14.554 --> 01:17.107
form a line that runs parallel to the trench.

01:17.107 --> 01:20.841
Japan, Indonesia, the Philippines and the Andes are all lines

01:20.841 --> 01:24.698
like this. Whether a volcano flows or explodes depends on its

01:24.698 --> 01:27.501
magma. Magma that is low in silica is runny.

01:27.501 --> 01:31.736
Gas bubbles escape from it easily, so the lava pours out and flows.

01:31.736 --> 01:34.476
That is what you see in Hawaii and Iceland.

01:34.476 --> 01:37.591
Magma that is high in silica is thick and sticky.

01:37.591 --> 01:41.452
Gas cannot escape, so the pressure builds, like a fizzy drink

01:41.452 --> 01:43.819
that has been shaken with the cap on.

01:43.819 --> 01:47.991
When it is finally released, the gas expands all at once and blows

01:47.991 --> 01:49.611
the magma apart into ash.

01:49.611 --> 01:52.725
Subduction zone magma is usually the sticky kind.

01:52.725 --> 01:56.524
That is why Pinatubo, Merapi and Vesuvius erupt explosively.

01:56.524 --> 01:59.819
Explosive volcanoes are dangerous in three main ways.

01:59.819 --> 02:01.446
The first is ash fall.

02:01.446 --> 02:04.018
Ash is tiny pieces of rock and glass.

02:04.018 --> 02:07.762
It collapses roofs, ruins crops, and stops aircraft.

02:07.762 --> 02:09.939
The second is pyroclastic flows.

02:09.939 --> 02:13.715
These are clouds of hot gas and ash that pour down the slopes

02:13.715 --> 02:15.527
faster than a car on a highway.

02:15.527 --> 02:17.348
Nothing in their path survives.

02:17.348 --> 02:20.723
They buried Pompeii in the year seventy-nine, and they killed

02:20.723 --> 02:23.752
people on the slopes of Merapi in two thousand ten.

02:23.752 --> 02:25.278
The third is lahars.

02:25.278 --> 02:29.861
A lahar is a mudflow of ash and water that rushes down river valleys.

02:29.861 --> 02:33.713
It can happen years after an eruption, in heavy rain.

02:33.713 --> 02:36.426
Lava flows, by comparison, are usually slow.

02:36.426 --> 02:39.445
They destroy property, but they rarely kill.

02:39.445 --> 02:42.022
Pinatubo shows what good monitoring can do.

02:42.022 --> 02:45.245
For weeks before the eruption there were warning signs:

02:45.245 --> 02:48.986
thousands of small earthquakes, steam explosions, ground that

02:48.986 --> 02:51.188
was swelling, and rising amounts of gas.

02:51.188 --> 02:54.207
Scientists from the Philippines and the United States watched

02:54.207 --> 02:56.339
all of this and mapped the danger zones.

02:56.339 --> 02:59.358
The authorities moved tens of thousands of people out.

02:59.358 --> 03:02.966
The main eruption came on the fifteenth of June, on the same day

03:02.966 --> 03:07.035
as a typhoon. Wet, heavy ash collapsed roofs, and several hundred

03:07.035 --> 03:10.703
people died. But the evacuation is thought to have saved at least

03:10.703 --> 03:11.888
five thousand lives.

03:11.888 --> 03:15.418
The ash and gas also spread around the world, and cooled the whole

03:15.418 --> 03:17.975
planet by about half a degree for the next two years.

03:17.975 --> 03:21.635
This is the big difference between volcanoes and earthquakes.

03:21.635 --> 03:23.761
Earthquakes arrive without warning.

03:23.761 --> 03:27.540
Volcanoes usually give one, because magma has to move before it

03:27.540 --> 03:31.614
can erupt. Scientists watch for small earthquakes under the volcano,

03:31.614 --> 03:35.393
for the ground swelling, for changes in the gases, and they use

03:35.393 --> 03:36.929
satellite images as well.

03:36.929 --> 03:40.648
At Merapi, in Indonesia, in two thousand ten, the warnings led

03:40.648 --> 03:44.486
to more than three hundred and fifty thousand people being moved

03:44.486 --> 03:48.265
out. About three hundred and fifty still died, and most of them

03:48.265 --> 03:51.218
had stayed, or gone back, inside the danger zone.

03:51.218 --> 03:54.997
So a warning only works if people are able to leave, and choose

03:54.997 --> 03:57.418
to. Check yourself with three questions.

03:57.418 --> 03:58.717
Pause if you need to.

03:58.717 --> 04:01.138
One. Mantle melts above a sinking plate.

04:01.138 --> 04:02.319
What makes it melt?

04:02.319 --> 04:04.858
Two. A magma is sticky and high in silica.

04:04.858 --> 04:06.865
Will it flow or explode, and why?

04:06.865 --> 04:10.703
Three. Heavy rain falls on a volcano two years after it erupted.

04:10.703 --> 04:13.419
Which hazard should people downstream expect?

04:13.419 --> 04:14.718
Here are the answers.

04:14.718 --> 04:17.966
Water from the sinking plate lowers the melting point.

04:17.966 --> 04:21.686
Sticky magma explodes, because it traps gas until the pressure

04:21.686 --> 04:23.162
is released all at once.

04:23.162 --> 04:25.229
And rain on old ash makes a lahar.

04:25.229 --> 04:26.823
Now go on to today's task.
